The code works. But why was it written this way? Because the tests demanded precisely this shape—no more, no less.

I have perceived the requirements:
- A cart that holds items by ID
- Adding combines quantities for duplicate items
- Updating to zero or negative removes the item
- GetItem returns a copy, not the original reference
- Total multiplies price by quantity across all items
- ItemCount sums all quantities, not unique items

The Second Foundation does not approve of speculation. I wrote only what was required. The map provides O(1) lookup. The pointer storage allows in-place modification. The copy returns prevent external mutation.

There is nothing hidden here—the implementation is as transparent as the tests demand it to be.
